<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brahmanas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brahmanas are Vedic prose texts, composed roughly between 900 and 700 BCE, that explain the meaning and correct performance of the sacrificial rituals of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>. Attached to particular Vedic schools, they include the Aitareya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmanas</a>, and they contain myths, accounts of kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janamejaya">Janamejaya</a> and early speculation that leads into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>. They are a key source for the society of the Kuru and Panchala realms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brahmanas.jpg" alt="Brahmanas" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1636_CE_Samaveda,_Sadvimsha_Brahmana_(Pa%C3%B1cavi%E1%B9%83%C5%9Babr%C4%81hma%E1%B9%87a_supplement),_Benares_Sanskrit_college,_Edward_Cowell_collection,_sample_i,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
